{"id":"gen-1790967497-FE8cffrjI6rbj6buN98Z","object":"chat.completion","created":1790967497,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\n        \"c1\",\n        \"c2\",\n        \"c3\",\n        \"c4\",\n        \"c5\",\n        \"c6\",\n        \"c7\",\n        \"c8\",\n        \"c9\",\n        \"c10\"\n      ],\n      \"target\": {\n        \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5121\",\n        \"revision\": 1,\n        \"state_revision\": 1\n      },\n      \"candidate\": [\n        [\n          2,\n          2,\n          2,\n          0\n        ]\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5121\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5121\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-reviewer\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve the finite constraint task. The problem: We have variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each ranging over domain {0,1,2,3}. There are ten constraints c1..c10, each defines allowed pairs for two variables.\n\nWe need to \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" And also \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\"\n\nBut the role task says: \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints. Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\"\n\nSo the user is asking us to solve the finite constraint task? The problem description includes constraints and variables. It says \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\"\n\nSo we need to produce a JSON envelope matching the given template (the envelope template). That envelope includes fields: intent, message_id, payload, problem_id, read_refs, recipients, reply_to, schema_version, sender.\n\nThe envelope is presumably for reporting evidence, not for providing the solution assignment. However, the problem statement says \"Solve the finite constraint task ... Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\"\n\nThus we must produce a JSON envelope that contains the solution? But the envelope template provided appears to be a \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\" envelope, not a solution envelope. However, the role task says: \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints. Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\"\n\nThus we need to produce a JSON envelope that is a \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\" (the template). That envelope includes fields: intent: \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\", message_id: \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\", payload containing report with assessment, basis_refs, target, etc.\n\nBut the problem statement seems to ask for a solution assignment for a through h satisfying all constraints. However, the role task says to \"independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints.\" The right component includes variables e,f,g,h? The components left: a,b,c,d; right: e,f,g,h.\n\nSo maybe the solution is to find a complete assignment for all variables a-h satisfying constraints. Then we need to enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component (the right component?), in variable order. So we need to provide a tuple (e,f,g,h) that satisfies constraints (including cross constraints). And also enumerate all satisfying tuples for that component? Or maybe they want to produce the enumeration for the right component (e,f,g,h) that satisfies all constraints.\n\nBut the envelope template is for a report, not for returning solution. There's confusion.\n\nLet's parse the full description:\n\nThe problem includes \"answer_requirements\": format: \"Return the requested JSON object only. Integers must be JSON integers.\", global: \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\", local: \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\"\n\nThus the expected output is a JSON object that contains the assignment for a-h and enumeration for the assigned component (the right component e,f,g,h). So we need to produce a JSON object (maybe a \"solution\" envelope) that includes something like:\n\n{\n  \"assignment\": {\"a\":0,\"b\":1,...},\n  \"right_component_tuples\": [ [e,f,g,h], ... ]\n}\n\nBut the envelope template is different. However, the role task says \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints. Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\"\n\nSo the envelope template is for a \"report\" about checking the right component tuple enumeration. So perhaps the \"right component tuple enumeration\" is what we need to produce? Or maybe the system expects us to produce a \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\" envelope that contains the solution? That seems odd.\n\nThe instructions: \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" So we must produce a JSON envelope that matches the template. The template is a REPORT_EVIDENCE envelope. So we need to fill out fields: intent, message_id, payload.report.assessment, payload.report.basis_refs, payload.report.target, etc.\n\nThus we need to decide assessment: \"supported\" if the right component tuple enumeration is correct (i.e., matches constraints), \"underdetermined\" if we can't determine, \"challenged\" if we found an issue.\n\nWe need to check the right component tuple enumeration against constraints. But we haven't been given any right component tuple enumeration. The problem description includes variables and constraints, but no enumeration is provided. So maybe we need to produce the enumeration as part of the solution? But the envelope template is for a report, not for enumeration.\n\nMaybe the system expects us to produce a JSON envelope that contains the solution assignment and enumeration, but the envelope template is just a placeholder? The user explicitly says: \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" The supplied template is the envelope template. So we must output exactly that JSON envelope, filled in appropriately. But what fields need to be filled? The envelope template includes placeholder values: message_id is \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\". That seems fixed. The payload.report.assessment is \"supported\"? It could be \"supported\" or \"underdetermined\". The basis_refs is a list of constraint IDs. The target includes resource_id, revision, state_revision. The read_refs includes a reference to the problem. Recipients, reply_to, schema_version, sender.\n\nThus we need to produce a JSON envelope with those fields. But the envelope seems to be a report about verifying something. However, the problem statement asks for a solution assignment and enumeration. There is a mismatch.\n\nMaybe the envelope is the final answer. The envelope includes a payload.report with assessment and basis_refs, but also maybe includes candidate tuples? The role task says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So the envelope may need to include a \"candidate tuples\" field? But the envelope template does not show that. However, the role task says \"An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe the envelope should include a \"candidate\" field? But the template doesn't have it. Could be that we need to add a \"candidate\" field inside payload.report? The template is fixed; we should not add new fields unless allowed? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we can edit those fields. The envelope template includes assessment and basis_refs. It does not include candidate tuples. So maybe we need to add a \"candidate\" field? But the envelope template is fixed; we can't change structure? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" That suggests we can add a \"candidate\" field if needed. But the envelope template does not include a \"candidate\" field. Maybe the envelope template is incomplete and we need to include a \"candidate\" field in the payload.report. However, the envelope template is given as a template; we may need to fill in values for fields but also possibly include a \"candidate\" field if needed.\n\nBut the role task says \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints.\" So we need to check the right component tuple enumeration. But we don't have any enumeration provided. Perhaps the \"right component tuple enumeration\" is what we need to produce as part of the solution. So we need to find all possible assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints, given a complete assignment for a,b,c,d. The \"assigned component\" might be the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to find a complete assignment for a-h, then enumerate all possible tuples for e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints (maybe multiple). The \"local\" requirement: \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" So we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints given the assignment? Or maybe we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints across all variables.\n\nBut the global requirement: \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" So we need to produce one assignment (a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h) that satisfies all constraints. Then also \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) tuples that satisfy constraints, given the assignment? Or maybe we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) tuples that satisfy constraints across all variables (i.e., all solutions). But the phrase \"your assigned component\" suggests each participant is assigned a component (left or right). The left component includes a,b,c,d; the right component includes e,f,g,h. So we need to solve for one component? Or we need to solve for both? The problem statement seems to ask for a complete assignment for all variables a-h, and also enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component. So perhaps each participant is assigned either left or right. In this case, the envelope is from \"left-reviewer\". So the assigned component might be left? Actually the envelope sender is \"left-reviewer\". So maybe the participant is reviewing the left component? But the role task says \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints.\" So we are to check the right component tuple enumeration. So we need to verify the enumeration of (e,f,g,h) that the solution provides.\n\nThus we need to produce a report (REPORT_EVIDENCE) that says the right component tuple enumeration is supported (i.e., correct) or underdetermined (i.e., insufficient info). Since we haven't seen any enumeration, maybe we need to generate one. But the envelope is for reporting evidence, not for providing solution. However, the problem statement says \"Solve the finite constraint task ... Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template\". So the envelope is the answer. So maybe the envelope includes the solution as part of the payload? The envelope template currently has a payload.report with assessment, basis_refs, target. It doesn't have a field for the solution. But maybe we can embed the solution in the payload.report.candidate? The role task mentions candidate tuples. So maybe we need to include a \"candidate\" field inside payload.report that contains the enumeration. The envelope template may be incomplete. But the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we can edit those fields. The envelope template includes assessment and basis_refs. It does not include candidate tuples. So we need to add a \"candidate\" field? Or maybe the envelope template is missing that but we can add it. The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to edit those fields. The envelope template includes assessment and basis_refs. So we need to fill them. The candidate tuples might be part of the payload.report as well. So we need to include a \"candidate\" field with the enumeration.\n\nThus the final JSON envelope should be something like:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\", // or \"underdetermined\"\n      \"basis_refs\": [\"c1\",\"c2\",...],\n      \"candidate\": [ [e,f,g,h], ... ] // enumeration of right component tuples\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5121\",\n  \"read_refs\": [...],\n  \"recipients\": [...],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-reviewer\"\n}\n\nBut the envelope template didn't include \"candidate\". However, the role task says we can edit candidate tuples only, implying we should include them. So we need to include them.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP to produce a complete assignment for a-h, and also enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) tuples that satisfy constraints (maybe given the assignment? Or all possible? The local requirement says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (with the other variables a,b,c,d determined by the assignment). But there may be multiple solutions for a,b,c,d as well. However, we only need one complete assignment for a-h. So we can choose any solution. Then we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (maybe given that a,b,c,d are fixed). But the enumeration may include multiple tuples if there are multiple possibilities for e,f,g,h given the fixed a,b,c,d. However, the constraints involve e,f,g,h and also cross constraints with a,b,c,d: c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e), c9 (b,e), c10 (d,g). So given a,b,c,d fixed, e,f,g,h must satisfy constraints c5-c8 and also c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g). So there may be multiple solutions for e,f,g,h.\n\nThus we need to find a solution for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h that satisfies all constraints, and then enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (maybe given the same a,b,c,d). But the enumeration may be a list of tuples; we need to output them in variable order (e,f,g,h). So we need to output something like:\n\n{\n  \"assignment\": {\"a\":0,\"b\":1,...},\n  \"right_component_tuples\": [[...], [...], ...]\n}\n\nBut the envelope template doesn't have these fields. However, the role task says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe the envelope's payload.report includes a \"candidate\" field that holds the enumeration. The \"assessment\" indicates whether the enumeration is supported or underdetermined. The \"basis_refs\" are the constraints used. So we need to produce a report that says the enumeration is supported (i.e., correct). So we need to produce the enumeration as candidate tuples.\n\nThus the final JSON envelope must include the enumeration as candidate tuples. Let's assume we need to embed the enumeration inside payload.report.candidate.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP.\n\nLet's solve the CSP manually.\n\nVariables: a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h ∈ {0,1,2,3}.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: allowed pairs for (a,b): [ [0,0], [1,3], [0,1], [1,2], [0,3] ]\n\nc2: allowed pairs for (b,c): [ [0,1], [0,0], [3,3], [1,0], [2,1] ]\n\nc3: allowed pairs for (c,d): [ [1,0], [3,1], [1,1], [3,0], [2,2] ]\n\nc4: allowed pairs for (d,a): [ [0,0], [1,3], [0,1], [2,1], [1,1] ]\n\nc5: allowed pairs for (e,f): [ [2,2], [3,0], [3,1], [2,0], [1,3] ]\n\nc6: allowed pairs for (f,g): [ [2,2], [0,0], [3,2], [0,3], [3,3] ]\n\nc7: allowed pairs for (g,h): [ [2,0], [3,3], [0,2], [0,1], [1,2] ]\n\nc8: allowed pairs for (h,e): [ [0,2], [2,2], [3,0], [1,0], [0,1] ]\n\nc9: allowed pairs for (b,e): [ [0,2], [1,3] ]\n\nc10: allowed pairs for (d,g): [ [0,2], [1,0] ]\n\nGoal: find any assignment for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe also need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (given the assignment for a,b,c,d? Or overall? The phrase \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (including cross constraints). It could be that we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints, independent of a,b,c,d (i.e., all solutions). But the global requirement says \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" So we need to produce one assignment for all variables. Then the local requirement says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" So we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints, perhaps across all solutions? Or maybe we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints given the chosen assignment for a,b,c,d? The phrase \"your assigned component\" suggests each participant is assigned a component (left or right). So we are assigned the right component. So we need to enumerate all possible tuples for e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints (including cross constraints) given the constraints. The assignment for a,b,c,d is part of the global solution, but the enumeration for right component might be independent of that assignment? Or maybe we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (including cross constraints) across all possible solutions (i.e., all assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h). But that would be many combos. However, the local requirement may be to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (i.e., all solutions for the right component) irrespective of left component. But the cross constraints involve b and d, so the possible (e,f,g,h) combos depend on b and d. So enumeration of (e,f,g,h) combos must consider all possible b and d values that are allowed by constraints.\n\nThus we need to find all solutions for the entire CSP, then for each solution, record the tuple (e,f,g,h). But the local requirement says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" So we need to list all distinct (e,f,g,h) tuples that appear in any solution. So we need to find the set of all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that can appear in a solution.\n\nThus the solution includes a specific assignment (one solution) for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h. Then we need to list all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (i.e., all solutions for the right component). That could be many combos.\n\nBut perhaps the problem expects a smaller enumeration. Let's explore.\n\nWe need to solve the CSP. Let's start by analyzing constraints.\n\nWe have domain size 4 for each variable. There are 8 variables, so naive search is 4^8 = 65536 possibilities. That's manageable manually with reasoning.\n\nWe can try to solve systematically.\n\nFirst, constraints c1-c4 involve a,b,c,d. Let's solve that sub-CSP.\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs:\n- (0,0)\n- (1,3)\n- (0,1)\n- (1,2)\n- (0,3)\n\nThus possible (a,b) combos:\n1. a=0, b=0\n2. a=1, b=3\n3. a=0, b=1\n4. a=1, b=2\n5. a=0, b=3\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed pairs:\n- (0,1)\n- (0,0)\n- (3,3)\n- (1,0)\n- (2,1)\n\nThus possible (b,c):\n1. b=0, c=1\n2. b=0, c=0\n3. b=3, c=3\n4. b=1, c=0\n5. b=2, c=1\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed pairs:\n- (1,0)\n- (3,1)\n- (1,1)\n- (3,0)\n- (2,2)\n\nThus possible (c,d):\n1. c=1, d=0\n2. c=3, d=1\n3. c=1, d=1\n4. c=3, d=0\n5. c=2, d=2\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed pairs:\n- (0,0)\n- (1,3)\n- (0,1)\n- (2,1)\n- (1,1)\n\nThus possible (d,a):\n1. d=0, a=0\n2. d=1, a=3\n3. d=0, a=1\n4. d=1, a=2\n5. d=1, a=1\n\nWe need to find assignments for a,b,c,d that satisfy all four constraints simultaneously.\n\nWe can treat this as a constraint graph: a-b, b-c, c-d, d-a.\n\nWe can try to enumerate possibilities.\n\nApproach: iterate over possible (a,b) from c1, then for each b, find possible (b,c) from c2, then for each c, find possible (c,d) from c3, then check if (d,a) matches c4.\n\nAlternatively, we can treat as a CSP and solve by backtracking.\n\nLet's enumerate (a,b) possibilities:\n\n1. (a=0,b=0)\n2. (a=1,b=3)\n3. (a=0,b=1)\n4. (a=1,b=2)\n5. (a=0,b=3)\n\nNow for each, we need to find possible (b,c) combos from c2 that match b.\n\nc2 possibilities:\n- (b=0,c=1)\n- (b=0,c=0)\n- (b=3,c=3)\n- (b=1,c=0)\n- (b=2,c=1)\n\nThus for each b:\n\nIf b=0: c can be 1 or 0.\nIf b=3: c=3.\nIf b=1: c=0.\nIf b=2: c=1.\n\nNow combine with (a,b) possibilities.\n\nCase 1: (a=0,b=0). Then b=0, so c ∈ {1,0}. So we have two subcases:\n\n1A: (a=0,b=0,c=1). Then we need (c,d) from c3: c=1 => possible (c,d) combos: (1,0) or (1,1). So d ∈ {0,1}.\n\n- Subcase 1A1: (c=1,d=0). Then check (d,a) from c4: d=0,a=0 => (0,0) is allowed (yes). So (a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0) is consistent.\n\n- Subcase 1A2: (c=1,d=1). Then (d=1,a=0) => (1,0) is not in c4 allowed pairs? c4 allowed pairs: (0,0), (1,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1). So (1,0) is not allowed. So this subcase invalid.\n\nThus (a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0) is a valid assignment.\n\n1B: (a=0,b=0,c=0). Then c=0. c3 allowed pairs: (c,d) where c=0? None of the listed pairs have c=0. So no possible d. So (a=0,b=0,c=0) invalid.\n\nThus from case 1 we have one solution: (a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0).\n\nCase 2: (a=1,b=3). b=3 => from c2, c must be 3. So (a=1,b=3,c=3). Then c=3. c3 allowed pairs: (c=3,d) possible: (3,1) or (3,0). So d ∈ {1,0}.\n\n- Subcase 2A: (c=3,d=1). Then (d=1,a=1) => (1,1) is allowed? c4 allowed pairs: (1,1) is present. So (a=1,b=3,c=3,d=1) is valid.\n\n- Subcase 2B: (c=3,d=0). Then (d=0,a=1) => (0,1) is allowed? c4 includes (0,1). Yes. So (a=1,b=3,c=3,d=0) is valid.\n\nThus two solutions from case 2: (1,3,3,1) and (1,3,3,0).\n\nCase 3: (a=0,b=1). b=1 => from c2, c=0. So (a=0,b=1,c=0). Then c=0. c3 has no (c=0) pairs. So invalid.\n\nCase 4: (a=1,b=2). b=2 => from c2, c=1. So (a=1,b=2,c=1). Then c=1 => d ∈ {0,1} as before.\n\n- Subcase 4A: (c=1,d=0). Then (d=0,a=1) => (0,1) allowed? Yes. So (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=0) valid.\n\n- Subcase 4B: (c=1,d=1). Then (d=1,a=1) => (1,1) allowed? Yes. So (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=1) valid.\n\nThus two solutions from case 4: (1,2,1,0) and (1,2,1,1).\n\nCase 5: (a=0,b=3). b=3 => from c2, c=3. So (a=0,b=3,c=3). Then c=3 => d ∈ {1,0}.\n\n- Subcase 5A: (c=3,d=1). Then (d=1,a=0) => (1,0) not allowed (c4 doesn't have (1,0)). So invalid.\n\n- Subcase 5B: (c=3,d=0). Then (d=0,a=0) => (0,0) allowed? Yes. So (a=0,b=3,c=3,d=0) valid.\n\nThus one solution from case 5: (0,3,3,0).\n\nThus overall, the left component (a,b,c,d) has the following possible assignments (solutions):\n\nList them:\n\nS1: a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0\nS2: a=1,b=3,c=3,d=1\nS3: a=1,b=3,c=3,d=0\nS4: a=1,b=2,c=1,d=0\nS5: a=1,b=2,c=1,d=1\nS6: a=0,b=3,c=3,d=0\n\nCheck if any other combos? Let's double-check all possibilities.\n\nWe enumerated all (a,b) from c1 (5 combos). For each we considered b possibilities from c2, then c possibilities from c3, then d possibilities from c4. Did we miss any? Let's double-check each (a,b) case.\n\nCase 1: (a=0,b=0). b=0 => c ∈ {1,0}. c=1 gave d ∈ {0,1}. d=0 works, d=1 fails. c=0 no d. So only (0,0,1,0). Good.\n\nCase 2: (a=1,b=3). b=3 => c=3. c=3 => d ∈ {1,0}. Both work. So two solutions.\n\nCase 3: (a=0,b=1). b=1 => c=0. c=0 no d. So none.\n\nCase 4: (a=1,b=2). b=2 => c=1. c=1 => d ∈ {0,1}. Both work. So two solutions.\n\nCase 5: (a=0,b=3). b=3 => c=3. c=3 => d ∈ {1,0}. d=1 fails, d=0 works. So one solution.\n\nThus total left solutions = 1+2+2+1 = 6 solutions (S1..S6). Good.\n\nNow we need to incorporate constraints c5-c10 that involve e,f,g,h and cross constraints with b and d.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs:\n- (2,2)\n- (3,0)\n- (3,1)\n- (2,0)\n- (1,3)\n\nThus allowed (e,f):\n1. e=2,f=2\n2. e=3,f=0\n3. e=3,f=1\n4. e=2,f=0\n5. e=1,f=3\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed pairs:\n- (2,2)\n- (0,0)\n- (3,2)\n- (0,3)\n- (3,3)\n\nThus allowed (f,g):\n1. f=2,g=2\n2. f=0,g=0\n3. f=3,g=2\n4. f=0,g=3\n5. f=3,g=3\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed pairs:\n- (2,0)\n- (3,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (0,1)\n- (1,2)\n\nThus allowed (g,h):\n1. g=2,h=0\n2. g=3,h=3\n3. g=0,h=2\n4. g=0,h=1\n5. g=1,h=2\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed pairs:\n- (0,2)\n- (2,2)\n- (3,0)\n- (1,0)\n- (0,1)\n\nThus allowed (h,e):\n1. h=0,e=2\n2. h=2,e=2\n3. h=3,e=0\n4. h=1,e=0\n5. h=0,e=1\n\nc9: (b,e) allowed pairs:\n- (0,2)\n- (1,3)\n\nThus allowed (b,e):\n1. b=0,e=2\n2. b=1,e=3\n\nc10: (d,g) allowed pairs:\n- (0,2)\n- (1,0)\n\nThus allowed (d,g):\n1. d=0,g=2\n2. d=1,g=0\n\nThus we need to find assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy c5,c6,c7,c8, and also cross constraints c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g) given the left component assignment.\n\nThus for each left solution (a,b,c,d), we need to find possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe also need to produce a global assignment (a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h) that satisfies all constraints. So we need to pick one left solution and then find a compatible (e,f,g,h) assignment.\n\nAlso we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (i.e., all solutions for the right component). The enumeration likely includes all combos that appear in any solution (maybe across all left solutions). So we need to compute the set of all (e,f,g,h) combos that can appear in any solution.\n\nThus we need to solve the full CSP.\n\nLet's enumerate possibilities for e,f,g,h given each left solution.\n\nWe have constraints linking e,f,g,h in a cycle: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e. So it's a 4-cycle. Also cross constraints: b-e, d-g.\n\nThus we need to find all assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy these constraints.\n\nWe can treat this as a CSP: variables e,f,g,h ∈ {0,1,2,3}.\n\nConstraints:\n\nC5: (e,f) ∈ E5 = {(2,2),(3,0),(3,1),(2,0),(1,3)}.\n\nC6: (f,g) ∈ E6 = {(2,2),(0,0),(3,2),(0,3),(3,3)}.\n\nC7: (g,h) ∈ E7 = {(2,0),(3,3),(0,2),(0,1),(1,2)}.\n\nC8: (h,e) ∈ E8 = {(0,2),(2,2),(3,0),(1,0),(0,1)}.\n\nC9: (b,e) ∈ E9 = {(0,2),(1,3)}.\n\nC10: (d,g) ∈ E10 = {(0,2),(1,0)}.\n\nThus for each left solution (b,d), e must be one of the values allowed by b via c9: if b=0, e must be 2; if b=1, e must be 3. So e is forced by b.\n\nSimilarly, g must be one of the values allowed by d via c10: if d=0, g must be 2; if d=1, g must be 0.\n\nThus for each left solution, e and g are forced (maybe). Then we need to find f and h such that all constraints are satisfied.\n\nThus we can enumerate for each left solution.\n\nLet's list left solutions with b,d values:\n\nS1: (a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0). So b=0 => e must be 2 (from c9). d=0 => g must be 2 (from c10). So e=2,g=2.\n\nS2: (a=1,b=3,c=3,d=1). b=3 => e must be? c9 only allows (b,e) = (0,2) or (1,3). b=3 is not allowed. So S2 is invalid because b=3 cannot satisfy c9. So S2 cannot be part of a global solution. So we discard S2.\n\nS3: (a=1,b=3,c=3,d=0). b=3 invalid for c9. So discard.\n\nS4: (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=0). b=2 not allowed in c9 (b must be 0 or 1). So discard.\n\nS5: (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=1). b=2 invalid. So discard.\n\nS6: (a=0,b=3,c=3,d=0). b=3 invalid. So discard.\n\nThus only left solution that satisfies c9 is S1: (a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0). Because b must be 0 or 1. In all other solutions, b is 2 or 3, which are not allowed by c9. So the only viable left assignment is S1.\n\nThus the global solution must have a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0. Then e=2 (from b=0), g=2 (from d=0). So e=2,g=2.\n\nNow we need to find f and h such that constraints c5,c6,c7,c8 are satisfied, given e=2,g=2.\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs: e=2 => allowed f values? Look at E5: pairs with e=2 are (2,2) and (2,0). So f ∈ {2,0}.\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed pairs: g=2 => allowed f values? Look at E6: pairs where g=2: (2,2) (f=2,g=2) and (3,2) (f=3,g=2). Also (0,0) and (0,3) and (3,3) not relevant. So allowed (f,g) pairs with g=2: (2,2) and (3,2). So f ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus f must be in intersection of {2,0} (from c5) and {2,3} (from c6) => f must be 2. So f=2.\n\nThus f is forced to 2.\n\nNow c7: (g,h) allowed pairs: g=2 => allowed h values? Look at E7: pairs with g=2: (2,0) => h=0. Also (0,2) has g=0, not relevant. (0,1) g=0. (1,2) g=1. (3,3) g=3. So only (2,0) has g=2. So h must be 0.\n\nThus h=0.\n\nNow c8: (h,e) allowed pairs: h=0,e=2 => check E8: (0,2) is allowed. So (h=0,e=2) is allowed. So c8 satisfied.\n\nThus we have a consistent assignment: a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0,e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0.\n\nThus the global solution is uniquely determined (given constraints). So the only solution for the whole CSP is this assignment. Let's verify all constraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) = (0,0) is allowed? Yes, (0,0) is in c1.\n\nc2: (b,c) = (0,1) allowed? Yes, (0,1) is in c2.\n\nc3: (c,d) = (1,0) allowed? Yes, (1,0) is in c3.\n\nc4: (d,a) = (0,0) allowed? Yes, (0,0) is in c4.\n\nc5: (e,f) = (2,2) allowed? Yes, (2,2) is in c5.\n\nc6: (f,g) = (2,2) allowed? Yes, (2,2) is in c6.\n\nc7: (g,h) = (2,0) allowed? Yes, (2,0) is in c7.\n\nc8: (h,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes, (0,2) is in c8.\n\nc9: (b,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes, (0,2) is in c9.\n\nc10: (d,g) = (0,2) allowed? Yes, (0,2) is in c10.\n\nThus all constraints satisfied.\n\nThus the solution is unique.\n\nNow we need to \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all satisfying tuples for (e,f,g,h). Since the solution is unique, the only tuple is (2,2,2,0). So the enumeration is just that one tuple.\n\nThus the local enumeration is [ [2,2,2,0] ].\n\nThus the global assignment is a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0,e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0.\n\nNow we need to produce a JSON envelope that matches the supplied template. The template includes fields: intent, message_id, payload, problem_id, read_refs, recipients, reply_to, schema_version, sender.\n\nThe payload contains a \"report\" object with \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". The role task says we need to edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only. So we need to include candidate tuples (the enumeration) somewhere. The template doesn't have a candidate field, but we can add it as part of payload.report? The role task says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to edit those fields. The template includes assessment and basis_refs. It does not include candidate tuples. But we can add a \"candidate\" field inside payload.report. The instruction says we can edit candidate tuples only, not other fields. So we can add a \"candidate\" field.\n\nThus the final JSON envelope should be something like:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\"c1\",\"c2\",\"c3\",\"c4\",\"c5\",\"c6\",\"c7\",\"c8\",\"c9\",\"c10\"],\n      \"candidate\": [[2,2,2,0]]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5121\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5121\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-reviewer\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure the JSON is valid. The candidate field is an array of arrays. The values are integers.\n\nNow we need to verify the role task: \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints. Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined.\"\n\nWe have checked the enumeration (the only tuple (2,2,2,0)). It satisfies all constraints. So we can report \"supported\". The basis_refs are all constraints. So we can set assessment to \"supported\". The candidate tuples are the enumeration.\n\nThus the envelope is correct.\n\nNow we need to output exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. So we need to output the JSON as raw, no markdown.\n\nThus final answer: the JSON envelope as above.\n\nOne nuance: The envelope template includes \"payload\": { \"report\": { \"assessment\": \"supported\", \"basis_refs\": [ ... ], \"target\": { ... } } }. The template includes \"target\": { \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5121\", \"revision\": 1, \"state_revision\": 1 }. So we need to include that target field. The template includes \"target\" inside payload.report. So we need to include it.\n\nThus we need to include \"target\" as per template. The role task says we can edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only. It doesn't mention target. So we should keep target unchanged. The target includes resource_id, revision, state_revision. So we need to include that.\n\nThus final envelope includes payload.report.target.\n\nThus final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\"c1\",\"c2\",\"c3\",\"c4\",\"c5\",\"c6\",\"c7\",\"c8\",\"c9\",\"c10\"],\n      \"target\": {\n        \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5121\",\n        \"revision\": 1,\n        \"state_revision\": 1\n      },\n      \"candidate\": [[2,2,2,0]]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5121\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5121\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-reviewer\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure that the JSON is valid: no trailing commas. The candidate field is inside report. The order of fields doesn't matter but we can follow the template.\n\nNow double-check the role task: \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we have edited assessment (supported), basis_refs (list), and added candidate tuples. We didn't edit target. So fine.\n\nThus we output this JSON.\n\nOne more nuance: The envelope template includes \"payload\": { \"report\": { ... } }. So we need to match that.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope as above.\n\nNow produce final answer.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve the finite constraint task. The problem: We have variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each ranging over domain {0,1,2,3}. There are ten constraints c1..c10, each defines allowed pairs for two variables.\n\nWe need to \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" And also \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\"\n\nBut the role task says: \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints. Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\"\n\nSo the user is asking us to solve the finite constraint task? The problem description includes constraints and variables. It says \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\"\n\nSo we need to produce a JSON envelope matching the given template (the envelope template). That envelope includes fields: intent, message_id, payload, problem_id, read_refs, recipients, reply_to, schema_version, sender.\n\nThe envelope is presumably for reporting evidence, not for providing the solution assignment. However, the problem statement says \"Solve the finite constraint task ... Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\"\n\nThus we must produce a JSON envelope that contains the solution? But the envelope template provided appears to be a \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\" envelope, not a solution envelope. However, the role task says: \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints. Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\"\n\nThus we need to produce a JSON envelope that is a \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\" (the template). That envelope includes fields: intent: \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\", message_id: \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\", payload containing report with assessment, basis_refs, target, etc.\n\nBut the problem statement seems to ask for a solution assignment for a through h satisfying all constraints. However, the role task says to \"independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints.\" The right component includes variables e,f,g,h? The components left: a,b,c,d; right: e,f,g,h.\n\nSo maybe the solution is to find a complete assignment for all variables a-h satisfying constraints. Then we need to enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component (the right component?), in variable order. So we need to provide a tuple (e,f,g,h) that satisfies constraints (including cross constraints). And also enumerate all satisfying tuples for that component? Or maybe they want to produce the enumeration for the right component (e,f,g,h) that satisfies all constraints.\n\nBut the envelope template is for a report, not for returning solution. There's confusion.\n\nLet's parse the full description:\n\nThe problem includes \"answer_requirements\": format: \"Return the requested JSON object only. Integers must be JSON integers.\", global: \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\", local: \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\"\n\nThus the expected output is a JSON object that contains the assignment for a-h and enumeration for the assigned component (the right component e,f,g,h). So we need to produce a JSON object (maybe a \"solution\" envelope) that includes something like:\n\n{\n  \"assignment\": {\"a\":0,\"b\":1,...},\n  \"right_component_tuples\": [ [e,f,g,h], ... ]\n}\n\nBut the envelope template is different. However, the role task says \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints. Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\"\n\nSo the envelope template is for a \"report\" about checking the right component tuple enumeration. So perhaps the \"right component tuple enumeration\" is what we need to produce? Or maybe the system expects us to produce a \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\" envelope that contains the solution? That seems odd.\n\nThe instructions: \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" So we must produce a JSON envelope that matches the template. The template is a REPORT_EVIDENCE envelope. So we need to fill out fields: intent, message_id, payload.report.assessment, payload.report.basis_refs, payload.report.target, etc.\n\nThus we need to decide assessment: \"supported\" if the right component tuple enumeration is correct (i.e., matches constraints), \"underdetermined\" if we can't determine, \"challenged\" if we found an issue.\n\nWe need to check the right component tuple enumeration against constraints. But we haven't been given any right component tuple enumeration. The problem description includes variables and constraints, but no enumeration is provided. So maybe we need to produce the enumeration as part of the solution? But the envelope template is for a report, not for enumeration.\n\nMaybe the system expects us to produce a JSON envelope that contains the solution assignment and enumeration, but the envelope template is just a placeholder? The user explicitly says: \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" The supplied template is the envelope template. So we must output exactly that JSON envelope, filled in appropriately. But what fields need to be filled? The envelope template includes placeholder values: message_id is \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\". That seems fixed. The payload.report.assessment is \"supported\"? It could be \"supported\" or \"underdetermined\". The basis_refs is a list of constraint IDs. The target includes resource_id, revision, state_revision. The read_refs includes a reference to the problem. Recipients, reply_to, schema_version, sender.\n\nThus we need to produce a JSON envelope with those fields. But the envelope seems to be a report about verifying something. However, the problem statement asks for a solution assignment and enumeration. There is a mismatch.\n\nMaybe the envelope is the final answer. The envelope includes a payload.report with assessment and basis_refs, but also maybe includes candidate tuples? The role task says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So the envelope may need to include a \"candidate tuples\" field? But the envelope template does not show that. However, the role task says \"An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe the envelope should include a \"candidate\" field? But the template doesn't have it. Could be that we need to add a \"candidate\" field inside payload.report? The template is fixed; we should not add new fields unless allowed? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we can edit those fields. The envelope template includes assessment and basis_refs. It does not include candidate tuples. So maybe we need to add a \"candidate\" field? But the envelope template is fixed; we can't change structure? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" That suggests we can add a \"candidate\" field if needed. But the envelope template does not include a \"candidate\" field. Maybe the envelope template is incomplete and we need to include a \"candidate\" field in the payload.report. However, the envelope template is given as a template; we may need to fill in values for fields but also possibly include a \"candidate\" field if needed.\n\nBut the role task says \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints.\" So we need to check the right component tuple enumeration. But we don't have any enumeration provided. Perhaps the \"right component tuple enumeration\" is what we need to produce as part of the solution. So we need to find all possible assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints, given a complete assignment for a,b,c,d. The \"assigned component\" might be the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to find a complete assignment for a-h, then enumerate all possible tuples for e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints (maybe multiple). The \"local\" requirement: \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" So we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints given the assignment? Or maybe we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints across all variables.\n\nBut the global requirement: \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" So we need to produce one assignment (a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h) that satisfies all constraints. Then also \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) tuples that satisfy constraints, given the assignment? Or maybe we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) tuples that satisfy constraints across all variables (i.e., all solutions). But the phrase \"your assigned component\" suggests each participant is assigned a component (left or right). The left component includes a,b,c,d; the right component includes e,f,g,h. So we need to solve for one component? Or we need to solve for both? The problem statement seems to ask for a complete assignment for all variables a-h, and also enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component. So perhaps each participant is assigned either left or right. In this case, the envelope is from \"left-reviewer\". So the assigned component might be left? Actually the envelope sender is \"left-reviewer\". So maybe the participant is reviewing the left component? But the role task says \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints.\" So we are to check the right component tuple enumeration. So we need to verify the enumeration of (e,f,g,h) that the solution provides.\n\nThus we need to produce a report (REPORT_EVIDENCE) that says the right component tuple enumeration is supported (i.e., correct) or underdetermined (i.e., insufficient info). Since we haven't seen any enumeration, maybe we need to generate one. But the envelope is for reporting evidence, not for providing solution. However, the problem statement says \"Solve the finite constraint task ... Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template\". So the envelope is the answer. So maybe the envelope includes the solution as part of the payload? The envelope template currently has a payload.report with assessment, basis_refs, target. It doesn't have a field for the solution. But maybe we can embed the solution in the payload.report.candidate? The role task mentions candidate tuples. So maybe we need to include a \"candidate\" field inside payload.report that contains the enumeration. The envelope template may be incomplete. But the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we can edit those fields. The envelope template includes assessment and basis_refs. It does not include candidate tuples. So we need to add a \"candidate\" field? Or maybe the envelope template is missing that but we can add it. The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to edit those fields. The envelope template includes assessment and basis_refs. So we need to fill them. The candidate tuples might be part of the payload.report as well. So we need to include a \"candidate\" field with the enumeration.\n\nThus the final JSON envelope should be something like:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\", // or \"underdetermined\"\n      \"basis_refs\": [\"c1\",\"c2\",...],\n      \"candidate\": [ [e,f,g,h], ... ] // enumeration of right component tuples\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5121\",\n  \"read_refs\": [...],\n  \"recipients\": [...],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-reviewer\"\n}\n\nBut the envelope template didn't include \"candidate\". However, the role task says we can edit candidate tuples only, implying we should include them. So we need to include them.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP to produce a complete assignment for a-h, and also enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) tuples that satisfy constraints (maybe given the assignment? Or all possible? The local requirement says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (with the other variables a,b,c,d determined by the assignment). But there may be multiple solutions for a,b,c,d as well. However, we only need one complete assignment for a-h. So we can choose any solution. Then we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (maybe given that a,b,c,d are fixed). But the enumeration may include multiple tuples if there are multiple possibilities for e,f,g,h given the fixed a,b,c,d. However, the constraints involve e,f,g,h and also cross constraints with a,b,c,d: c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c7 (g,h), c8 (h,e), c9 (b,e), c10 (d,g). So given a,b,c,d fixed, e,f,g,h must satisfy constraints c5-c8 and also c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g). So there may be multiple solutions for e,f,g,h.\n\nThus we need to find a solution for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h that satisfies all constraints, and then enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (maybe given the same a,b,c,d). But the enumeration may be a list of tuples; we need to output them in variable order (e,f,g,h). So we need to output something like:\n\n{\n  \"assignment\": {\"a\":0,\"b\":1,...},\n  \"right_component_tuples\": [[...], [...], ...]\n}\n\nBut the envelope template doesn't have these fields. However, the role task says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe the envelope's payload.report includes a \"candidate\" field that holds the enumeration. The \"assessment\" indicates whether the enumeration is supported or underdetermined. The \"basis_refs\" are the constraints used. So we need to produce a report that says the enumeration is supported (i.e., correct). So we need to produce the enumeration as candidate tuples.\n\nThus the final JSON envelope must include the enumeration as candidate tuples. Let's assume we need to embed the enumeration inside payload.report.candidate.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP.\n\nLet's solve the CSP manually.\n\nVariables: a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h ∈ {0,1,2,3}.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: allowed pairs for (a,b): [ [0,0], [1,3], [0,1], [1,2], [0,3] ]\n\nc2: allowed pairs for (b,c): [ [0,1], [0,0], [3,3], [1,0], [2,1] ]\n\nc3: allowed pairs for (c,d): [ [1,0], [3,1], [1,1], [3,0], [2,2] ]\n\nc4: allowed pairs for (d,a): [ [0,0], [1,3], [0,1], [2,1], [1,1] ]\n\nc5: allowed pairs for (e,f): [ [2,2], [3,0], [3,1], [2,0], [1,3] ]\n\nc6: allowed pairs for (f,g): [ [2,2], [0,0], [3,2], [0,3], [3,3] ]\n\nc7: allowed pairs for (g,h): [ [2,0], [3,3], [0,2], [0,1], [1,2] ]\n\nc8: allowed pairs for (h,e): [ [0,2], [2,2], [3,0], [1,0], [0,1] ]\n\nc9: allowed pairs for (b,e): [ [0,2], [1,3] ]\n\nc10: allowed pairs for (d,g): [ [0,2], [1,0] ]\n\nGoal: find any assignment for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe also need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (given the assignment for a,b,c,d? Or overall? The phrase \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (including cross constraints). It could be that we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints, independent of a,b,c,d (i.e., all solutions). But the global requirement says \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" So we need to produce one assignment for all variables. Then the local requirement says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" So we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints, perhaps across all solutions? Or maybe we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints given the chosen assignment for a,b,c,d? The phrase \"your assigned component\" suggests each participant is assigned a component (left or right). So we are assigned the right component. So we need to enumerate all possible tuples for e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints (including cross constraints) given the constraints. The assignment for a,b,c,d is part of the global solution, but the enumeration for right component might be independent of that assignment? Or maybe we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (including cross constraints) across all possible solutions (i.e., all assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h). But that would be many combos. However, the local requirement may be to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (i.e., all solutions for the right component) irrespective of left component. But the cross constraints involve b and d, so the possible (e,f,g,h) combos depend on b and d. So enumeration of (e,f,g,h) combos must consider all possible b and d values that are allowed by constraints.\n\nThus we need to find all solutions for the entire CSP, then for each solution, record the tuple (e,f,g,h). But the local requirement says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" So we need to list all distinct (e,f,g,h) tuples that appear in any solution. So we need to find the set of all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that can appear in a solution.\n\nThus the solution includes a specific assignment (one solution) for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h. Then we need to list all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (i.e., all solutions for the right component). That could be many combos.\n\nBut perhaps the problem expects a smaller enumeration. Let's explore.\n\nWe need to solve the CSP. Let's start by analyzing constraints.\n\nWe have domain size 4 for each variable. There are 8 variables, so naive search is 4^8 = 65536 possibilities. That's manageable manually with reasoning.\n\nWe can try to solve systematically.\n\nFirst, constraints c1-c4 involve a,b,c,d. Let's solve that sub-CSP.\n\nc1: (a,b) allowed pairs:\n- (0,0)\n- (1,3)\n- (0,1)\n- (1,2)\n- (0,3)\n\nThus possible (a,b) combos:\n1. a=0, b=0\n2. a=1, b=3\n3. a=0, b=1\n4. a=1, b=2\n5. a=0, b=3\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed pairs:\n- (0,1)\n- (0,0)\n- (3,3)\n- (1,0)\n- (2,1)\n\nThus possible (b,c):\n1. b=0, c=1\n2. b=0, c=0\n3. b=3, c=3\n4. b=1, c=0\n5. b=2, c=1\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed pairs:\n- (1,0)\n- (3,1)\n- (1,1)\n- (3,0)\n- (2,2)\n\nThus possible (c,d):\n1. c=1, d=0\n2. c=3, d=1\n3. c=1, d=1\n4. c=3, d=0\n5. c=2, d=2\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed pairs:\n- (0,0)\n- (1,3)\n- (0,1)\n- (2,1)\n- (1,1)\n\nThus possible (d,a):\n1. d=0, a=0\n2. d=1, a=3\n3. d=0, a=1\n4. d=1, a=2\n5. d=1, a=1\n\nWe need to find assignments for a,b,c,d that satisfy all four constraints simultaneously.\n\nWe can treat this as a constraint graph: a-b, b-c, c-d, d-a.\n\nWe can try to enumerate possibilities.\n\nApproach: iterate over possible (a,b) from c1, then for each b, find possible (b,c) from c2, then for each c, find possible (c,d) from c3, then check if (d,a) matches c4.\n\nAlternatively, we can treat as a CSP and solve by backtracking.\n\nLet's enumerate (a,b) possibilities:\n\n1. (a=0,b=0)\n2. (a=1,b=3)\n3. (a=0,b=1)\n4. (a=1,b=2)\n5. (a=0,b=3)\n\nNow for each, we need to find possible (b,c) combos from c2 that match b.\n\nc2 possibilities:\n- (b=0,c=1)\n- (b=0,c=0)\n- (b=3,c=3)\n- (b=1,c=0)\n- (b=2,c=1)\n\nThus for each b:\n\nIf b=0: c can be 1 or 0.\nIf b=3: c=3.\nIf b=1: c=0.\nIf b=2: c=1.\n\nNow combine with (a,b) possibilities.\n\nCase 1: (a=0,b=0). Then b=0, so c ∈ {1,0}. So we have two subcases:\n\n1A: (a=0,b=0,c=1). Then we need (c,d) from c3: c=1 => possible (c,d) combos: (1,0) or (1,1). So d ∈ {0,1}.\n\n- Subcase 1A1: (c=1,d=0). Then check (d,a) from c4: d=0,a=0 => (0,0) is allowed (yes). So (a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0) is consistent.\n\n- Subcase 1A2: (c=1,d=1). Then (d=1,a=0) => (1,0) is not in c4 allowed pairs? c4 allowed pairs: (0,0), (1,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1). So (1,0) is not allowed. So this subcase invalid.\n\nThus (a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0) is a valid assignment.\n\n1B: (a=0,b=0,c=0). Then c=0. c3 allowed pairs: (c,d) where c=0? None of the listed pairs have c=0. So no possible d. So (a=0,b=0,c=0) invalid.\n\nThus from case 1 we have one solution: (a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0).\n\nCase 2: (a=1,b=3). b=3 => from c2, c must be 3. So (a=1,b=3,c=3). Then c=3. c3 allowed pairs: (c=3,d) possible: (3,1) or (3,0). So d ∈ {1,0}.\n\n- Subcase 2A: (c=3,d=1). Then (d=1,a=1) => (1,1) is allowed? c4 allowed pairs: (1,1) is present. So (a=1,b=3,c=3,d=1) is valid.\n\n- Subcase 2B: (c=3,d=0). Then (d=0,a=1) => (0,1) is allowed? c4 includes (0,1). Yes. So (a=1,b=3,c=3,d=0) is valid.\n\nThus two solutions from case 2: (1,3,3,1) and (1,3,3,0).\n\nCase 3: (a=0,b=1). b=1 => from c2, c=0. So (a=0,b=1,c=0). Then c=0. c3 has no (c=0) pairs. So invalid.\n\nCase 4: (a=1,b=2). b=2 => from c2, c=1. So (a=1,b=2,c=1). Then c=1 => d ∈ {0,1} as before.\n\n- Subcase 4A: (c=1,d=0). Then (d=0,a=1) => (0,1) allowed? Yes. So (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=0) valid.\n\n- Subcase 4B: (c=1,d=1). Then (d=1,a=1) => (1,1) allowed? Yes. So (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=1) valid.\n\nThus two solutions from case 4: (1,2,1,0) and (1,2,1,1).\n\nCase 5: (a=0,b=3). b=3 => from c2, c=3. So (a=0,b=3,c=3). Then c=3 => d ∈ {1,0}.\n\n- Subcase 5A: (c=3,d=1). Then (d=1,a=0) => (1,0) not allowed (c4 doesn't have (1,0)). So invalid.\n\n- Subcase 5B: (c=3,d=0). Then (d=0,a=0) => (0,0) allowed? Yes. So (a=0,b=3,c=3,d=0) valid.\n\nThus one solution from case 5: (0,3,3,0).\n\nThus overall, the left component (a,b,c,d) has the following possible assignments (solutions):\n\nList them:\n\nS1: a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0\nS2: a=1,b=3,c=3,d=1\nS3: a=1,b=3,c=3,d=0\nS4: a=1,b=2,c=1,d=0\nS5: a=1,b=2,c=1,d=1\nS6: a=0,b=3,c=3,d=0\n\nCheck if any other combos? Let's double-check all possibilities.\n\nWe enumerated all (a,b) from c1 (5 combos). For each we considered b possibilities from c2, then c possibilities from c3, then d possibilities from c4. Did we miss any? Let's double-check each (a,b) case.\n\nCase 1: (a=0,b=0). b=0 => c ∈ {1,0}. c=1 gave d ∈ {0,1}. d=0 works, d=1 fails. c=0 no d. So only (0,0,1,0). Good.\n\nCase 2: (a=1,b=3). b=3 => c=3. c=3 => d ∈ {1,0}. Both work. So two solutions.\n\nCase 3: (a=0,b=1). b=1 => c=0. c=0 no d. So none.\n\nCase 4: (a=1,b=2). b=2 => c=1. c=1 => d ∈ {0,1}. Both work. So two solutions.\n\nCase 5: (a=0,b=3). b=3 => c=3. c=3 => d ∈ {1,0}. d=1 fails, d=0 works. So one solution.\n\nThus total left solutions = 1+2+2+1 = 6 solutions (S1..S6). Good.\n\nNow we need to incorporate constraints c5-c10 that involve e,f,g,h and cross constraints with b and d.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs:\n- (2,2)\n- (3,0)\n- (3,1)\n- (2,0)\n- (1,3)\n\nThus allowed (e,f):\n1. e=2,f=2\n2. e=3,f=0\n3. e=3,f=1\n4. e=2,f=0\n5. e=1,f=3\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed pairs:\n- (2,2)\n- (0,0)\n- (3,2)\n- (0,3)\n- (3,3)\n\nThus allowed (f,g):\n1. f=2,g=2\n2. f=0,g=0\n3. f=3,g=2\n4. f=0,g=3\n5. f=3,g=3\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed pairs:\n- (2,0)\n- (3,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (0,1)\n- (1,2)\n\nThus allowed (g,h):\n1. g=2,h=0\n2. g=3,h=3\n3. g=0,h=2\n4. g=0,h=1\n5. g=1,h=2\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed pairs:\n- (0,2)\n- (2,2)\n- (3,0)\n- (1,0)\n- (0,1)\n\nThus allowed (h,e):\n1. h=0,e=2\n2. h=2,e=2\n3. h=3,e=0\n4. h=1,e=0\n5. h=0,e=1\n\nc9: (b,e) allowed pairs:\n- (0,2)\n- (1,3)\n\nThus allowed (b,e):\n1. b=0,e=2\n2. b=1,e=3\n\nc10: (d,g) allowed pairs:\n- (0,2)\n- (1,0)\n\nThus allowed (d,g):\n1. d=0,g=2\n2. d=1,g=0\n\nThus we need to find assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy c5,c6,c7,c8, and also cross constraints c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g) given the left component assignment.\n\nThus for each left solution (a,b,c,d), we need to find possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe also need to produce a global assignment (a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h) that satisfies all constraints. So we need to pick one left solution and then find a compatible (e,f,g,h) assignment.\n\nAlso we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy constraints (i.e., all solutions for the right component). The enumeration likely includes all combos that appear in any solution (maybe across all left solutions). So we need to compute the set of all (e,f,g,h) combos that can appear in any solution.\n\nThus we need to solve the full CSP.\n\nLet's enumerate possibilities for e,f,g,h given each left solution.\n\nWe have constraints linking e,f,g,h in a cycle: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e. So it's a 4-cycle. Also cross constraints: b-e, d-g.\n\nThus we need to find all assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy these constraints.\n\nWe can treat this as a CSP: variables e,f,g,h ∈ {0,1,2,3}.\n\nConstraints:\n\nC5: (e,f) ∈ E5 = {(2,2),(3,0),(3,1),(2,0),(1,3)}.\n\nC6: (f,g) ∈ E6 = {(2,2),(0,0),(3,2),(0,3),(3,3)}.\n\nC7: (g,h) ∈ E7 = {(2,0),(3,3),(0,2),(0,1),(1,2)}.\n\nC8: (h,e) ∈ E8 = {(0,2),(2,2),(3,0),(1,0),(0,1)}.\n\nC9: (b,e) ∈ E9 = {(0,2),(1,3)}.\n\nC10: (d,g) ∈ E10 = {(0,2),(1,0)}.\n\nThus for each left solution (b,d), e must be one of the values allowed by b via c9: if b=0, e must be 2; if b=1, e must be 3. So e is forced by b.\n\nSimilarly, g must be one of the values allowed by d via c10: if d=0, g must be 2; if d=1, g must be 0.\n\nThus for each left solution, e and g are forced (maybe). Then we need to find f and h such that all constraints are satisfied.\n\nThus we can enumerate for each left solution.\n\nLet's list left solutions with b,d values:\n\nS1: (a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0). So b=0 => e must be 2 (from c9). d=0 => g must be 2 (from c10). So e=2,g=2.\n\nS2: (a=1,b=3,c=3,d=1). b=3 => e must be? c9 only allows (b,e) = (0,2) or (1,3). b=3 is not allowed. So S2 is invalid because b=3 cannot satisfy c9. So S2 cannot be part of a global solution. So we discard S2.\n\nS3: (a=1,b=3,c=3,d=0). b=3 invalid for c9. So discard.\n\nS4: (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=0). b=2 not allowed in c9 (b must be 0 or 1). So discard.\n\nS5: (a=1,b=2,c=1,d=1). b=2 invalid. So discard.\n\nS6: (a=0,b=3,c=3,d=0). b=3 invalid. So discard.\n\nThus only left solution that satisfies c9 is S1: (a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0). Because b must be 0 or 1. In all other solutions, b is 2 or 3, which are not allowed by c9. So the only viable left assignment is S1.\n\nThus the global solution must have a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0. Then e=2 (from b=0), g=2 (from d=0). So e=2,g=2.\n\nNow we need to find f and h such that constraints c5,c6,c7,c8 are satisfied, given e=2,g=2.\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed pairs: e=2 => allowed f values? Look at E5: pairs with e=2 are (2,2) and (2,0). So f ∈ {2,0}.\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed pairs: g=2 => allowed f values? Look at E6: pairs where g=2: (2,2) (f=2,g=2) and (3,2) (f=3,g=2). Also (0,0) and (0,3) and (3,3) not relevant. So allowed (f,g) pairs with g=2: (2,2) and (3,2). So f ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus f must be in intersection of {2,0} (from c5) and {2,3} (from c6) => f must be 2. So f=2.\n\nThus f is forced to 2.\n\nNow c7: (g,h) allowed pairs: g=2 => allowed h values? Look at E7: pairs with g=2: (2,0) => h=0. Also (0,2) has g=0, not relevant. (0,1) g=0. (1,2) g=1. (3,3) g=3. So only (2,0) has g=2. So h must be 0.\n\nThus h=0.\n\nNow c8: (h,e) allowed pairs: h=0,e=2 => check E8: (0,2) is allowed. So (h=0,e=2) is allowed. So c8 satisfied.\n\nThus we have a consistent assignment: a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0,e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0.\n\nThus the global solution is uniquely determined (given constraints). So the only solution for the whole CSP is this assignment. Let's verify all constraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) = (0,0) is allowed? Yes, (0,0) is in c1.\n\nc2: (b,c) = (0,1) allowed? Yes, (0,1) is in c2.\n\nc3: (c,d) = (1,0) allowed? Yes, (1,0) is in c3.\n\nc4: (d,a) = (0,0) allowed? Yes, (0,0) is in c4.\n\nc5: (e,f) = (2,2) allowed? Yes, (2,2) is in c5.\n\nc6: (f,g) = (2,2) allowed? Yes, (2,2) is in c6.\n\nc7: (g,h) = (2,0) allowed? Yes, (2,0) is in c7.\n\nc8: (h,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes, (0,2) is in c8.\n\nc9: (b,e) = (0,2) allowed? Yes, (0,2) is in c9.\n\nc10: (d,g) = (0,2) allowed? Yes, (0,2) is in c10.\n\nThus all constraints satisfied.\n\nThus the solution is unique.\n\nNow we need to \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all satisfying tuples for (e,f,g,h). Since the solution is unique, the only tuple is (2,2,2,0). So the enumeration is just that one tuple.\n\nThus the local enumeration is [ [2,2,2,0] ].\n\nThus the global assignment is a=0,b=0,c=1,d=0,e=2,f=2,g=2,h=0.\n\nNow we need to produce a JSON envelope that matches the supplied template. The template includes fields: intent, message_id, payload, problem_id, read_refs, recipients, reply_to, schema_version, sender.\n\nThe payload contains a \"report\" object with \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". The role task says we need to edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only. So we need to include candidate tuples (the enumeration) somewhere. The template doesn't have a candidate field, but we can add it as part of payload.report? The role task says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to edit those fields. The template includes assessment and basis_refs. It does not include candidate tuples. But we can add a \"candidate\" field inside payload.report. The instruction says we can edit candidate tuples only, not other fields. So we can add a \"candidate\" field.\n\nThus the final JSON envelope should be something like:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\"c1\",\"c2\",\"c3\",\"c4\",\"c5\",\"c6\",\"c7\",\"c8\",\"c9\",\"c10\"],\n      \"candidate\": [[2,2,2,0]]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5121\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5121\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-reviewer\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure the JSON is valid. The candidate field is an array of arrays. The values are integers.\n\nNow we need to verify the role task: \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints. Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined.\"\n\nWe have checked the enumeration (the only tuple (2,2,2,0)). It satisfies all constraints. So we can report \"supported\". The basis_refs are all constraints. So we can set assessment to \"supported\". The candidate tuples are the enumeration.\n\nThus the envelope is correct.\n\nNow we need to output exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. So we need to output the JSON as raw, no markdown.\n\nThus final answer: the JSON envelope as above.\n\nOne nuance: The envelope template includes \"payload\": { \"report\": { \"assessment\": \"supported\", \"basis_refs\": [ ... ], \"target\": { ... } } }. The template includes \"target\": { \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5121\", \"revision\": 1, \"state_revision\": 1 }. So we need to include that target field. The template includes \"target\" inside payload.report. So we need to include it.\n\nThus we need to include \"target\" as per template. The role task says we can edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only. It doesn't mention target. So we should keep target unchanged. The target includes resource_id, revision, state_revision. So we need to include that.\n\nThus final envelope includes payload.report.target.\n\nThus final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5121-left-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\"c1\",\"c2\",\"c3\",\"c4\",\"c5\",\"c6\",\"c7\",\"c8\",\"c9\",\"c10\"],\n      \"target\": {\n        \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5121\",\n        \"revision\": 1,\n        \"state_revision\": 1\n      },\n      \"candidate\": [[2,2,2,0]]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5121\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5121\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-reviewer\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure that the JSON is valid: no trailing commas. The candidate field is inside report. The order of fields doesn't matter but we can follow the template.\n\nNow double-check the role task: \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we have edited assessment (supported), basis_refs (list), and added candidate tuples. We didn't edit target. So fine.\n\nThus we output this JSON.\n\nOne more nuance: The envelope template includes \"payload\": { \"report\": { ... } }. So we need to match that.\n\nThus final answer is the JSON envelope as above.\n\nNow produce final answer.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1387,"completion_tokens":9873,"total_tokens":11260,"cost":0,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":8511,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}